Z||000000||KevinP||08-12-2000||05:27 PM||||Last week I undertook the job of replacing all of those little things that needed work; rear calipers and rotors, shocks, struts, parking brake, etc.  In the process, my "assistant" accidentally ripped the boot on the outboard side of the passenger side driveshaft. After I threw my wrenches at the wall (I considered throwing them at the car, or my assistant, but the wall was closest), I thought that maybe I could just make a patch with some liquid rubber.  The problem is, the tear is pretty big (about 2 inches) so I might need to replace the boot.  Has anybody ever done this? Any Advice?  All input appreciated.<BR>Latta'<BR>   Kevin||||reg||9
Z||000001||GT Bastard||08-12-2000||07:46 PM||||You can buy something called a quick-boot that allows you to replace the boot without having to remove the axle.  I'd rather just use a regular boot though, because it's not hard to get to the axle.||||reg||1
